<p class="gna-lede">If your jaw suddenly will not open more than about two finger-widths — often just after a click you have had for months goes quiet — the most likely cause is a <strong>closed lock</strong>: the small cushioning disc inside the jaw joint has slipped forward and is no longer slipping back into place. A closed lock is uncomfortable and worrying but rarely dangerous, and many people improve with simple, non-surgical care. It is quite different from an <strong>open lock</strong>, where the mouth is stuck open and you need urgent help.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-closed-lock">What is a closed lock?</h2>
<p>Each jaw joint (temporomandibular joint, or TMJ) sits just in front of the ear. The rounded upper end of the lower jaw, called the condyle, glides forward in a shallow socket in the skull every time you open wide. Between the two bones sits a small, flexible disc of fibrous tissue that works a little like a shock absorber.</p>
<p>In many people the disc sits slightly too far forward. When it slips back onto the condyle as the mouth opens, you hear or feel a <strong>click</strong>. Doctors call this <em>disc displacement with reduction</em>. It is very common and often needs no treatment at all. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/herniated-tmj-jaw-clicking-locking-or-pain-heres-what-it-really-means/">jaw clicking, locking and pain</a> explains this stage in more detail.</p>
<p>In a closed lock, the disc stays in front of the condyle and acts like a doorstop. The condyle cannot glide forward past it, so the mouth opens only partly. The medical term is <em>disc displacement without reduction, with limited opening</em>. Some people first have brief episodes of catching that release on their own (intermittent locking) before a lock becomes persistent.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A closed lock typically limits opening to around 20–30 mm, roughly two finger-widths; comfortable normal opening is usually about 40–50 mm.</li>
    <li>It often follows months or years of clicking, and the click usually disappears when the lock starts.</li>
    <li>A closed lock is rarely an emergency; an open lock, where the mouth is stuck open, is.</li>
    <li>Treatment usually starts with conservative care. Manipulation, arthrocentesis or arthroscopy are options if the jaw does not improve.</li>
    <li>Early assessment — within days to a few weeks — keeps more options open.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="closed-vs-open-lock">Closed lock vs open lock: what is the difference?</h2>
<p>People often say "lockjaw" for any stuck jaw, but the two main kinds of jaw-joint locking are very different problems.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Closed lock</th><th>Open lock (dislocation)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>What happens</td><td>The disc blocks the condyle from moving forward</td><td>The condyle slides too far forward, past a bony ridge, and gets stuck</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Mouth position</td><td>Opens only partly; you can still bring your teeth together</td><td>Stuck open; you cannot close your mouth</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Typical trigger</td><td>Often none; sometimes noticed on waking or after a wide yawn or hard bite</td><td>A wide yawn, long dental appointment, vomiting, laughing hard or a seizure</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Urgency</td><td>Book a specialist assessment within days</td><td>Urgent — go to an emergency department</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Usual first treatment</td><td>Soft diet, anti-inflammatories, splint, physiotherapy; manipulation in selected cases</td><td>Repositioning by a clinician, sometimes with sedation or muscle relaxation</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>With an open lock, do not let anyone force the jaw back with heavy pressure. Repositioning should be done by a trained clinician. If dislocations keep happening, there are treatments to reduce recurrence, so mention any previous episodes.</p>

<h2 id="symptoms">Signs that point to a closed lock</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Sudden or gradual inability to open the mouth fully — a large bite of food or a toothbrush at the back teeth becomes difficult</li>
  <li>A clicking sound that had been present for some time and has now stopped</li>
  <li>The jaw swinging towards one side as you open, usually towards the affected joint</li>
  <li>Difficulty moving the jaw towards the opposite side</li>
  <li>Pain in front of the ear, especially when trying to open wider or chew</li>
  <li>A feeling that something is blocking the jaw — a firm stop rather than a gradual tightness</li>
</ul>
<p>Pain levels vary widely. Some people have a stiff but fairly comfortable jaw; others have significant aching that disturbs eating and sleep.</p>

<h2 id="other-causes">Not every stiff jaw is a closed lock</h2>
<p>Limited mouth opening has many causes, which is why an examination matters before any treatment. Other possibilities include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Muscle spasm</strong> of the chewing muscles from clenching, stress or a long dental procedure — opening usually feels tight rather than blocked.</li>
  <li><strong>Infection</strong> around a partly erupted wisdom tooth or a dental abscess, often with swelling, fever or a bad taste.</li>
  <li><strong>Oral submucous fibrosis</strong>, a gradual stiffening of the lining of the mouth linked to chewing areca nut, gutka or paan masala. It is common in India and important to recognise early — read more about <a href="/blogs/oral-submucous-fibrosis-causes-symptoms-treatment/">oral submucous fibrosis</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>A fracture</strong> after a fall, road-traffic accident or assault — see our guide to <a href="/blogs/broken-jaw-symptoms/">broken jaw symptoms</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Arthritis</strong> of the joint or, rarely, fusion of the joint (ankylosis) or a growth in the area.</li>
</ul>
<p>Tetanus can also cause jaw stiffness after a wound. It is rare when vaccinations are up to date, but it is a medical emergency.</p>

<h2 id="what-to-do">What to do if your jaw locks closed</h2>
<p>If your jaw has locked but you can breathe, swallow and bring your teeth together normally, a few sensible steps can help while you arrange an appointment:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Do not force it.</strong> Avoid prising the mouth open with your fingers or objects, and avoid repeated attempts to "pop" it back.</li>
  <li><strong>Switch to a soft diet</strong> — idli, upma, khichdi, curd rice, soups, soft dal-rice — and cut food into small pieces.</li>
  <li><strong>Use warmth.</strong> A warm compress over the joint and cheek for 10–15 minutes a few times a day can ease muscle guarding.</li>
  <li><strong>Consider simple pain relief</strong> such as paracetamol or an anti-inflammatory if it is safe for you. Check with a pharmacist or doctor first if you have stomach, kidney or heart problems, are pregnant, or take other medicines.</li>
  <li><strong>Avoid chewing gum, hard foods and very wide yawns</strong>, and notice whether you are clenching your teeth during the day.</li>
  <li><strong>Book an assessment</strong> with a maxillofacial surgeon or a dentist experienced in jaw-joint problems within days rather than weeks, especially if the lock started recently.</li>
</ol>

