<h2 id="treatment">How a specialist treats a closed lock</h2>
<p>Treatment depends on how long the jaw has been locked, how much it hurts, what imaging shows and how much the lock affects daily life. A typical plan moves step by step from the simplest options. You can read about the full range on our <a href="/tmj-treatment">TMJ treatment page</a>.</p>
<h3>Examination and imaging</h3>
<p>We measure your mouth opening, check side-to-side movement and your bite, and examine the joints and chewing muscles. An MRI scan is the test that shows where the disc is and whether the joint is inflamed; a CBCT or CT scan shows the bone. Imaging is not always needed straight away — it is ordered when the result is likely to change the plan.</p>
<h3>Manipulation</h3>
<p>If the lock is recent, the clinician may try to guide the disc back by gently drawing the jaw down and forward, sometimes after local anaesthetic to relax the area. This is more likely to work in the first days to weeks. Even when it succeeds, the disc may slip again, so a splint and exercises usually follow.</p>
<h3>Conservative care</h3>
<p>A custom splint worn at night, a short course of anti-inflammatory medicine, physiotherapy with guided stretching, and a soft diet form the backbone of treatment. Studies that have followed people with closed lock over months to years suggest that many regain comfortable, functional opening with this approach, even though the disc usually stays displaced — the tissues around it gradually adapt.</p>
<h3>Arthrocentesis</h3>
<p>If opening and pain do not improve, or the lock is very limiting, <a href="/blogs/tmj-arthrocentesis-explained/">arthrocentesis, a needle wash-out of the joint</a>, is often the next step. Sterile fluid is flushed through the upper part of the joint to remove inflammatory substances and loosen mild adhesions. A meta-analysis of a small number of studies found greater improvement in opening and pain with arthrocentesis than with splints alone in this condition, although the evidence base is still limited.</p>
<h3>Arthroscopy and open surgery</h3>
<p>When a wash-out does not help, TMJ arthroscopy lets the surgeon look inside the joint with a fine camera, release adhesions and treat inflamed tissue. Open joint surgery to reposition or remove the disc is reserved for a small number of people with persistent problems despite other treatment.</p>

<h2 id="outlook">Recovery and outlook</h2>
<p>The outlook for most people with a closed lock is reassuring. Opening and pain often improve over weeks to months, whether through conservative care alone or with a procedure. The click usually does not return, because the disc no longer slips back onto the condyle. For most people that does not matter, as long as the jaw opens comfortably.</p>
<p>Results vary. Some people are left with slightly reduced opening or occasional stiffness, and a minority develop wear-and-tear changes in the joint over time. Procedures carry their own risks — temporary swelling and bite changes and, uncommonly, infection or temporary facial nerve weakness — which your surgeon should explain before you decide. Surgery is one option among several, not the default.</p>
<p>Reducing load on the joint over the long term helps lower the chance of flare-ups: managing clenching and grinding, avoiding very hard foods, and keeping a relaxed posture during long hours at a screen or on the phone.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist — and when it is urgent</h2>
<p>Book an assessment if your jaw has locked and is not releasing, if opening is getting steadily worse, or if pain is affecting eating or sleep. Ask for a prompt review if limited opening appears without any history of clicking, or comes with numbness of the lip or chin, a lump near the jaw or ear, a white or red patch in the mouth, or stiffness inside the cheeks — these need examination to rule out other conditions.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department if:</strong> your mouth is stuck open and you cannot close it; your jaw stiffened after an injury, or your teeth no longer meet properly after a fall or accident; you have swelling of the face or neck with fever, difficulty swallowing or difficulty breathing; or jaw stiffness follows a wound and comes with muscle spasms elsewhere in the body. For facial injuries, our 24/7 facial-trauma line is +91 90006 66476.</p>
